  "summary": "Victorian-industrial futurism: brass, gears, steam engines, corsets over top hats, an alternate 19th century that mechanized beautifully.",
  "description": "Coined by K.W. Jeter (1987) for Verne-and-Wells-lineage fiction, steampunk grew into a full design culture: maker-built brass machines, goggles, airship pinions, clockwork keyboards and neo-Victorian dress. Its thesis — an ornate, anachronistic machine age — informs fashion, maker communities, games (Bioshock Infinite's Columbia is adjacent) and countless conventions. Sub-flavors: clockpunk (Renaissance), gaslamp fantasy, dieselpunk (its interwar successor).",
